<https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Who exactly is reading this, and what do they already know about the subject?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Which terms here did I learn inside my team or field, and have I replaced or defined each one where it first appears?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Could a reader say in their own words what I want them to do next?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-03"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Principle> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Plain language" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "clear language" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "plain English" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "plain writing" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/clarity> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The principle of writing so that the intended readers can find what they need, understand it and use it the first time they read it, by writing for that audience in familiar words, clear structure and direct sentences." .
